Why service layer matters
Because operations like assign item actually mean:

check item exists
check item is available
update the item’s status, assignee, and job site
insert a transaction

That is business logic, not just SQL.

Example service operations you should have
JavaaddNewItem(Item item)assignItem(int itemId, String assignedTo, String jobSite, String notes)returnItem(int itemId, String notes)retireItem(int itemId, String notes)changeStatus(int itemId, ItemStatus status, String notes)getInventory()searchInventory(String keyword)getTransactionsBetween(String start, String end)Show more lines

Critical backend rule: item update + transaction insert should happen together
This is one of the most important design points.
If you assign an item, you should:

update the item
create a transaction

If one succeeds and the other fails, your data becomes inconsistent.
So ideally, use a SQL transaction.

Example concept
For assignItem():
Javapublic void assignItem(int itemId, String assignedTo, String jobSite, String notes) {    // 1. find item    // 2. confirm it's available    // 3. update item status to ASSIGNED    // 4. update assignedTo + jobSite    // 5. add transaction of type CHECKED_OUT}``Show more lines

Better version: do it atomically
Inside DB code:
